<?xml version='1.0' standalone='yes'?>
<!DOCTYPE PLUGIN [
  <!ENTITY name      "tollroll">
  <!ENTITY slug      "tollroll">
  <!ENTITY author    "Junker der Provinz">
  <!ENTITY version   "1.0.1">
  <!ENTITY launch    "Settings/tollroll">
  <!ENTITY gitURL    "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/junkerderprovinz/tollroll/main/plugin">
  <!ENTITY pluginURL "&gitURL;/&slug;.plg">
  <!ENTITY pkgURL    "https://github.com/junkerderprovinz/tollroll/releases/download/v&version;">
  <!ENTITY emhttp    "/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&slug;">
  <!ENTITY plgPath   "/boot/config/plugins/&slug;">
  <!ENTITY txz       "&slug;-&version;-x86_64-1.txz">
]>

<PLUGIN name="&name;"
        author="&author;"
        version="&version;"
        launch="&launch;"
        pluginURL="&pluginURL;"
        min="6.12.0"
        support="https://github.com/junkerderprovinz/tollroll/issues">

<!-- The name is the lowercase slug so Unraid finds the logo
     (plugins/<slug>/images/<slug>.png) and the Plugins tab description
     (plugins/<slug>/README.md); the display name comes from README.md. -->
<ICON>
/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&slug;/images/&slug;.png
</ICON>

<CHANGES>
One dashboard tile for the apps of your fortress: a row per app with how it is
doing, and one log with the lines of all of them, filtered by a click. It joins
the fortress with its phrase and reads the status report every member serves.

### Unreleased
Your Unraid server gets a place on the tile: its syslog in plain words, the disks that woke up and why, and a watch for anything unusual. Thanks to @Revan335 for asking about it in [#3](https://github.com/junkerderprovinz/tollroll/issues/3).

✨ **Added**
- Unraid's syslog joins the log, sorted into errors, warnings and info. The lines that matter most, such as disk errors, parity checks, the mover, sign-ins or running out of memory, come with one short sentence that says what they mean. Hover a line to see the original.
- Routine noise like Docker's network chatter folds into one line, and a click shows it.
- A Disks row tells you which disk woke up, how often today and in the last 7 days, and the likely cause: a container, a VM, an SMB or NFS client, the mover, a parity check or a scheduled task. When TollRoll can't tell, it says so. It only reads, so it never wakes a disk itself.
- The Unraid row watches for the unusual: a sign-in from an address it has not seen before, a burst of failed sign-ins, a new plugin, changed settings on the flash drive, a new scheduled task, a share newly open to guests, and many files deleted at once.
- When /var/log is 80% full, the Unraid row names the biggest file and who writes it. At 90% you get one Unraid notification.
- The Unraid and Disks rows work without a fortress too. Until TollRoll joins one, they sit above the prompt to join.

🌐 **Translations**
- Every new text in all 26 languages Unraid offers.

### 1.0.1
The Help card leads to Discord, and with TowerTorch every row and tab lights in its hover colour.

✨ **Added**
- The Help card offers the TollRoll Discord server beside GitHub.

🎨 **Design**
- GlimStone 4.5.4. With TowerTorch, the rows, chips, views, options and tabs of the tile and the settings page light in TowerTorch's hover colour, a soft tint of your accent, the same as TowerTorch's own rows and buttons.

🌐 **Translations**
- The Help sentence names GitHub and Discord in all 26 languages, and French readers are addressed with tu.

### 1.0.0
Your whole fortress on one dashboard tile, with one log for every app. Joining takes twelve words, and you never type an address.

✨ **Added**
- A dashboard tile with a row per app of your fortress: its state, what needs you, what it is working on and its version. An app that runs on several devices still gets one row.
- One log with the lines of every app, newest at the bottom. Click an app, or its button above the log, and the log shows only its lines.
- Open in the tile header shows the chosen app's status window, with what needs you, what it is working on, what comes next and its latest log lines. For the app's own pages, open it in BombVault, KnightLoader or ArrowLoop, wherever you are.
- Your other apps can open TollRoll's status window too. It shows TollRoll's version, its next check and a log of what it told you.
- Join your fortress with its phrase under Settings → Utilities → TollRoll. Every app in the same fortress then shows up by itself, whether it runs on this server or on another device.
- Your other apps show TollRoll with its own logo.
- When your fortress moves to a new phrase, TollRoll follows on its own and stays the same member, so no duplicate shows up. If it can't, the settings page asks for the new words.
- If another app takes TollRoll out of the fortress, the settings page says so, after a restart too, and once the phrase has changed, lets it back in with the new one.
- Take any other member out of the fortress right from the settings page. TollRoll asks first, then points you to an app where you change the phrase, because until you do, your fortress takes no new app. Until then, a quiet line names who you took out, and an app that tries to join is told why.
- Four views in the tile header: everything, the apps alone, the log alone and a map of every device with its apps. The tile keeps your view and your pick of apps on the server, so every browser finds it the way you left it.
- Notifications through Unraid, Apprise or Matrix when an app stops answering, answers again or newly needs you. Each one goes out once, so a long outage does not fill your inbox.
- Every setting is saved the moment you change it. Only the twelve words wait for the Join button.
- TollRoll keeps only the keys made from your phrase, never the phrase itself. The browser talks to the Unraid WebGUI alone. At home the other apps reach the service behind it directly, relay or not, on a port that lets in fortress members and nobody else.

🎨 **Design**
- Each row wears the app's own logo and colour from its status report, or its first letter when the report has none. A logo is checked and only ever drawn as an image, so nothing in it can run.
- The log keeps its dark terminal look on every Unraid theme.
- The settings page has the same tabs as our other Unraid plugins: General, Notifications, Fortress and Info, with the version, help and a way to say thanks.
- The fortress card is the same in every one of our Unraid plugins and has its own tab, right above Info. It shows each app with its logo, how it runs and its version, and Open tells you how a plugin is doing.

🌐 **Translations**
- Every text in all 26 languages Unraid offers. Texts from your apps arrive in your language too, as far as each app has them.
</CHANGES>

<!-- Pre-install: stop the service, then prune packages of older versions only
     and keep the current one cached. On a reboot Unraid reinstalls each .plg
     before the network is up, so the cached &txz; on the flash is what lets the
     reinstall work offline. mkdir -p first, because on a fresh install the
     directory does not exist yet and find would end the install. -->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
&emhttp;/scripts/rc.tollroll stop 2>/dev/null
mkdir -p &plgPath;
find &plgPath; -maxdepth 1 -name '*.txz' ! -name '&txz;' -delete 2>/dev/null || true
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<!-- The package (service binary and webgui files) is built by
     plugin/pkg_build.sh and attached to the GitHub release "v&version;". CI
     writes the SHA256 at release. upgradepkg removes files a newer package
     dropped. -->
<FILE Name="&plgPath;/&txz;" Run="/sbin/upgradepkg --install-new --reinstall">
  <URL>&pkgURL;/&txz;</URL>
  <SHA256>4f431fa18ee0d70b88b658e3ce31704c4c07b1e11dcb53a7bbb0c4dbcb2643dc</SHA256>
</FILE>

<!-- Post-install: start the service. It runs on every boot as well, since
     Unraid reinstalls every plugin then. -->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
chmod -R u+rwX,go+rX &emhttp; 2>/dev/null
chmod +x &emhttp;/bin/&slug; &emhttp;/scripts/rc.tollroll 2>/dev/null
&emhttp;/scripts/rc.tollroll start
echo ""
cat &emhttp;/banner.txt 2>/dev/null
echo ""
echo "TollRoll is ready!"
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<!-- Remove. The plugin manager runs only FILE elements and ignores a bare
     REMOVE, and the block has to end with exit 0, or Unraid keeps the .plg and
     reinstalls it on boot. The settings on the flash stay, so a reinstall
     keeps the fortress membership and the notification channels. -->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="remove">
<INLINE>
cat &emhttp;/banner.txt 2>/dev/null
echo ""
echo "Bye!"
echo ""
&emhttp;/scripts/rc.tollroll stop 2>/dev/null
pkg=$(ls /var/log/packages/ 2>/dev/null | grep -E "^&slug;(-[^-]+-[^-]+-[^-]+)?$" | head -n1)
if [ -n "$pkg" ]; then removepkg "$pkg" 2>/dev/null; fi
rm -rf &emhttp;
rm -f &plgPath;/*.txz /var/run/&slug;.pid /var/run/&slug;.sock
echo "TollRoll removed. Your fortress membership and notification channels in"
echo "&plgPath; were kept. Delete that folder if you do not plan to reinstall."
exit 0
</INLINE>
</FILE>

</PLUGIN>
